{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1395197-adventure-ho","id":1395197,"title":"Adventure Ho!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-06-08T18:54:35-07:00","id":1395197,"title":"Adventure Ho!","content":"So I decided to put together some quick adventures since a lot of people have problems making fun and challenging encounters for their PCs. I tried to make these as generic as possible to let people fill in their own requirements, or modify them easily as needed.<br /> <br /> I also don't know what your group is, and what serves them best, so they may need to be tweaked. If you want them easier that's super simple, if you want them harder you can bump some of the challenges, but I recommend instead to just run two of them back to back. For example maybe something the PCs need in supposed to be in Fortress Run, but when they get there they find the calling card of a long dead Exalted thief, who stole it and put it in their nearby temple for Jungle Hunt. You only get back one willpower a night and wounds take days or even weeks to heal, so the second adventure should be harder regardless.<br /> <br /> Or maybe even three in a row, they found out what they needed at the gala, time is super critical so they went right to the fortress, and then oh my god even more lost time they need to Jungle Hunt.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fortress Run is where the players are told something they need is hidden deep in a temple, a mistake, it's actually a small first age fortress that's survived since the Usurpation.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Dawn of Claws</b><br /> <br /> Early in the morning, just as the cloudy sky is beginning to lighten, and with a light rain through the forest a group of eight claw striders approach to attack the group. Any character who is awake this early may make a difficulty 3 (Perception + Awareness) to notice their approach, with a -3 penalty due to poor lighting and very dense foliage.<br /> <br /> The striders will tend to strike for decisive attacks whenever they can, and will typically flee after five have been killed or wounded, although adjust as is appropriate for the situation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Spirit Door</b><br /> <br /> After some travel the heroes come upon the temple. Guarding the door to the temple is a spirit, bound by the creator of the fortress, who dematerializes and appears to the characters. Its purpose is to only allow the Solar who built the place and her designated underlings to pass.<br /> <br /> The spirit has Resolve 4, Guile 4, 10 dice to Social Influence, seven willpower, and 80 motes of essence. It's intimacies are<br /> <br /> Defining &quot;I brook no unauthorized passage.&quot;<br /> Major The Fallen World (Sadness)<br /> Minor Spirits on the other side of the door (affection)<br /> <br /> It also has a charm 10m 1wp to raise its resolve by three against any attempt to persuade it to open its door, and another for 10m 1wp that allows it to raise its guile by three against a Read Intentions roll, unless the character reveals an intimacy of similar magnitude to the spirit.<br /> <br /> It also attempts to get the characters to leave this temple and never return.<br /> <br /> <br /> The door can also be bashed open with a Strength 10, difficulty 15 feat of strength. If the spirit is engaged in combat use the stats of a field guardian. The first strike against it slays it as a failsafe against forcible entry and a set of blast doors slam down over the main ones, increasing the difficulty to bash through by 5.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Hall of Doors</b><br /> <br /> In the grand hall of the fortress all of the interior doors have been sealed. A case scene roll of difficulty 3 reveals hastily scribed symbols somewhere around each door, take a -2 penalty unless very bright light has been brought inside. During the Usurpation sabotage by revolutionary forces caused a breakdown in many of the magical spells wound into this place, these symbols were put in place to warn the revolutionaries what lay behind each.<br /> <br /> The symbols are not written in any language, but a shorthand of ancient military jargon. To decipher the code an Intelligence + Linguistics roll of difficulty 5 is required. Doing so reveals what doors are safe, dangerous, and which leads downward.<br /> <br /> If the players select a door at random, roll a d10 on the following chart:<br /> <br /> 1-5: Deadly gas, all characters within Short range are subject to a one time 3L, Difficulty 5 environmental hazard, resisted by Stamina + Resistance<br /> <br /> 6-9: Safe, feel free to add any intriguing thing, or simply empty worn room.<br /> <br /> 10: The door down.<br /> <br /> <br /> All doors require a lockpicking roll of difficulty 3 to access, with the way down being difficulty 4. Failure on the roll results in a release of toxic gas, but may be attempted again.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 Spiders Nest</b><br /> <br /> Behind the door down is a ten foot wide spiral staircase, however after two turns the stonework simply stops, having crumbled into the darkness below. Shining a light or tossing a torch down reveals a fall equal to Medium range, and onto the jagged remains of the stairwell. A difficulty 3 (Perception + Awareness) roll reveals that the blocks are covered in spiderwebs.<br /> <br /> The first character to make it to the bottom is subject to an Anhule's Web Spinning Snare trap, supplemented with Hell Silk Weaver. At this point seven Anhule crawl from cracks and overturned stone slabs at the perimeter of the cavernous room and attack. Characters take a -2 penalty to physical actions and defense due to uneven and webbed terrain.<br /> <br /> After the battle it takes a difficulty 3 Perception + Investigation roll to discover the hidden door out, obscured by layer after layer of thick webs and debris on the wall.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Sprung Trap</b><br /> <br /> Opening the door reveals an old defense mechanism that's already been activated. A wide hallway with a low ceiling extends forward, but several feet into the room the floor drops away, revealing a deep pit, Medium range down and Short range across. The bottom is absolutely covered with obsidian spikes and the bones and armor of dead revolutionaries, preserved from being dust only by the tightly sealed nature of the fortress.<br /> <br /> If the players can safely lower themselves to the floor of the trap, it takes a difficulty 4 Dexterity + Athletics roll to safely navigate across the twisted mass of razor sharp rock without taking 4 dice of lethal damage, ignoring hardness. The terrain itself is also difficult terrain, without charms or other effects to ignore that it takes two movement actions to cross it, and provokes two rolls to avoid damage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Fun with Magma</b><br /> <br /> Opening the door at the far end of the hallway reveals the vault room. At the far end there is a large door with three locks, with difficulty 4, 5 and 6. Opening any lock without the key, which has been destroyed centuries ago, triggers spillways to open and begin flooding the room with molten lava. Each character can take one action before it flows to the center of the room and covers the entire floor. The lockpicker can avoid triggering this trap by beating the difficulty of the lock by two.<br /> <br /> Inside the vault is whatever treasure the characters have come for, as well as a mechanism to cool the lava and allow them to walk back out.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> The Gala party assumes the players have some stake in a local fishery or fishing industry, along with a Great House throwing a get-together with the eye to convince your family to sell it to them.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Invitation Mixup</b><br /> <br /> On approach to the high palace they are stopped at the outer gate and asked for invitations. Apparently the invitations changed styles recently, and these old invitations are no longer valid. The guard has Appearance 3 guile 2 and resolve 4, with the following intimacies.<br /> <br /> Defining Host House (Loyalty)<br /> Major Immaculate Faith (devotion)<br /> Minor Tobacco (Addiction)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Introductions</b><br /> <br /> At the grand hall the characters are introduced to the host, a powerful dragonblooded socialite. She has Appearance 5, Resolve 4 (+2) Guile 5 (+2), and 10(+5) dice to social actions. The following intimacies:<br /> <br /> Defining:<br /> The bold sieze power and are rewarded for it<br /> Craftsmen and workers from the Blessed Isle are unmatched in the world<br /> <br /> Major:<br /> House (Loyalty)<br /> Husband (Love)<br /> I love exotic delights, clothing, and foods from far away<br /> <br /> Minor:<br /> Even Exalted life is too short not to enjoy<br /> Rival House (Fear and respect)<br /> Nephew (Pride)<br /> <br /> <br /> She also has Auspicious First Meeting Attitude, Vivacious Dragon Beauty, Loyalty Reading Meditation, Sweeten the Tap Method, Wildfire Scandal Revelation, and Rippling Mirror Face.<br /> <br /> At the entrance hallway the host is there to greet the newcomers as they arrive, and probe the important ones for what they may be up to. She's conservative with her motes and willpower here unless pressed, and certainly making a note of who presses her.<br /> <br /> She also introduces the players to her nephew, who currently oversees the weaving houses in the town below. He plans on being the one to oversee the new fishing industry if his aunt can seize it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Strike the Band</b><br /> <br /> The nephew invites the characters with him to one of the middle tiers of this many-tiered party, built along the mountain slope with several terraces and balconies all over. In this scene a band, with 9 dice to the roll, rolls to inspire the emotion of wonder.<br /> <br /> The nephew's goal here is to instill the intimacy that he's an excellent manager, talking about how he has taken the weavers from a small few houses into a flourishing and diverse industry. He has appearance 4, guile 4, resolve 3, 8(+4) dice to social actions, and otherwise the stats of a young dynast.<br /> <br /> He has Warm Faced Seduction Style, Passion Transmuting Nuance, Grinding Millstone Argument, and Auspicious First Meeting Attitude.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 The Duel</b><br /> <br /> One of the NPCs calls for a show duel to demonstrate the strength of the dynasty. Use the stats for the Earth immaculate in the corebook for this exhibition fight. Only one fight will take place and any PC can put themselves up for it. If they win everyone who sees it is treated as having a minor tie of respect for them. Killing the monk instead of just forcing them to surrender or knocking them out is considered extremely poor form, and suspicious, most people viewing the fight would become angry and receive a minor tie of disgust instead.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Dark Balcony</b><br /> <br /> After the duel concludes another character gets the PC's attention. A visiting noble from one of the host house's satrapies, a Dragonblood of the cadet house there. She has appearance 3, guile 4(+2), and resolve 3(+2). She asks to meet one or more of the characters on a secluded balcony off to the outskirts of the party. A successful Read Intentions roll reveals that she is genuine about the offer, and not leading them into any kind of a trap.<br /> <br /> Her intimacies are:<br /> <br /> Major Host House(Wary respect)<br /> Major Satrapy(Pride)<br /> Minor Nephew(Distain)<br /> <br /> This cadet noble has a proposition for the players. Her family has recently been having trouble. Some time ago their satrapy had produced some of the finest and most intricate woven fabrics in the world, with many patterns and techniques known exclusively to the people of her nation. Recently however the nephew poached almost all of her best workers with a combination of a silver tongue and massive funds. She now hopes to get them back. Either by ruining the weaving industry on the Blessed Isle and poaching them back, or by ruining his reputation and getting somebody else put in his place who is more amenable to her.<br /> <br /> If the players help her do this, she will cut them a deal for those textiles, and owe them a favour. Her goal here is to get the players to agree to this plan, and failing that convince them she is no threat to them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Big Moment</b><br /> <br /> Having set all of this up the Host goes in for the finish. She directs the band to inspire everyone with a song of hope and optimism as she approaches the players. She will then engage them in social influence, attempting to get them to agree that moving forward the best option would be for her to give their family large sums of money in exchange for them transferring control of their fishery companies to her very capable nephew, who she believes they've spent some time talking to.<br /> <br /> If she is not successful she holds no grudges, and simply recognizes the players as equal masters of the game, thinking that next time they shall not be so lucky.<br /> <br /> <b>Optional Scene Wyld Hunt</b><br /> <br /> If the characters are playing Anathema and have either been indiscreet lately, or simply by random unluck, a pair of Shikari may be at the party. The first is an amazingly skilled sanxian player, who takes up with the band for a single song first. She uses Audience Enthusing Display, Soul-Stirring Performance, and Talented Improvisation to try and inspire paranoia among everyone with 9 dice plus 5 for an excellency, also adding a willpower if this is her only mission for a few days. Any character swayed by this inspiration takes a -1 penalty to thier guile as their fears bubble to the surface.<br /> <br /> Immediately after that her compatriot arrives at the highest balcony overlooking the whole party. Players may make a Perception + Socialize roll at difficulty 5 to notice her before she activates her charms, on a success they do not suffer the penalty for being observed by a character they are unaware of. She has a similar pool to her ally and uses Clear-Eyed Courtier's Scrutiny with Eye for Passions Scrutiny to determine what everyone at the party wants from the scene. She notes any characters who have suspicious motives, like simply to get out of the party alive, or an intimacy of fear or hatred for the immaculate order. If anyone is very suspicious she moves to press them further with Rumour-Dredging Gaze, uncovering their most desperately hidden intimacy.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Jungle Hunt is an actual temple, once used to store books of great wisdom from some Shogunate era civilization, or perhaps Dragon Kings. The temple has rotted away from the inside and is surrounded by the worst kind of jungle.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Merely a fleabite</b><br /> <br /> As the characters get closer to their target the insects become more and more aggressive. An intelligence survival check of difficulty 3 reveals a way to avoid them using a local grub squashed and rubbed on the skin as a bug repellent. If the survival roll fails, or if anyone opts not to take this option, have them roll to resist Hemorrhagic Fever as a terrible insect born disease infects them.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Death Blossom</b><br /> <br /> On approach to the temple another intelligence survival roll, difficulty 3, is prompted by seeing massive white flowers and tangled vines surrounding the whole temple. These flowers have a unique defense mechanism to use against any birds or mammals, when disturbed by anything larger than a mouse they emit a burst of arsenic laden pollen into the air. If inhaled it counts as ingestion.<br /> <br /> If the players wish to hold their breath and run through the field treat it as an extended Dexterity + Athletics action with an interval of one minute, a difficulty of 2, and a target number of 10. This roll is made at a -3 penalty due to the difficult terrain. If characters are unable to make it all the way while holding their breath they are dosed with arsenic each interval that they continue through it.<br /> <br /> Characters with strength 3+ can make a difficulty 3 Feat of Strength roll to carry one other character with them, although this exact number and strength required may vary for exceptionally heavily equipped or light characters, as well as multiple characters.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Old Guards</b><br /> <br /> Once through the flowers exploding poison all over them, the happen upon the low, wide opening of the temple. Strangely, although it's open to the elements, it's clean and well maintained. That is because the custodians of this place still come alive at night to tend to it, and defend it. They do so now. A size 2 battlegroup of Walking Statues lead by a two-headed leader statue. The leader has a 10 die command pool and can flurry a command action with any other action with no penalties to either.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 Puzzle Lock</b><br /> <br /> Behind the golem guardians and in the hallway lies a large mandala carved into the floor, and no other visible exits. This mandala has a secret though, if enough people stand at the correct spots it opens to reveal a passage. A lore roll of difficulty 3 can be used to introduce a fact which tells the players where to stand. For example the cardinal directions being important to this culture, or perhaps ten, to and six on the clock face.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Getting Wet</b><br /> <br /> The mandala splits apart to reveal...a pool of water. Once this was a wide staircase that led down, now it is submerged. A survival intelligence roll of difficulty 3 reveals that one of the objects bobbing to the surface is an egg casing of some kind of large fish.<br /> <br /> As the characters swim down into the water they may notice with a perception + awareness roll of difficulty 2 that the bottom of the temple has crumbled into an underground and under water cave. A group of seven Benthic Knifetooth sharks at Long range below them roll to notice them as they slip into the water. They take a -3 penalty to their vision through the dark murky water, but ignore one point of it due to their night vision ability. If the players are using any kind of light they succeed automatically.<br /> <br /> The knifetooths then swim towards the players, using Depth Lurker and spending willpower to try and stay hidden until they ambush. In combat the knifetooth's priority is to keep the characters under the water, they know what easy prey drowned corpses can be.<br /> <br /> If the players bring an exceptionally strong light underwater with them reduce the visual penalty to spot the sharks to -2. Even characters who spot the monsters must make a Wits + Linguistics roll of difficulty 3 to communicate this information to their less observant allies, or use charms such as Speech Without Words or Poetic Expression of Style.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Time to Dry Off</b><br /> <br /> Depending on how the fight went some characters may now be drowning. To return them to the surface at their destination an extended Athletics + Strength roll is required, difficulty 0, target of 10, and interval of one round. Once back at the surface an Wits + Medicine roll is required to revive the dying character, at a difficulty equal to the rounds spent drowning. if those rounds exceed their (Stamina) all hope is lost, and the character is dead.<br /> <br /> The characters who surface here find themselves in an enclosed stone room, lit with several torches all around the perimeter, most of which burn with a rich red flame. At the center of the room is a tall stone pillar with a doorway etched into the side. As the last player breaches the surface and climbs out of the pool the doorway begins to glow and with a flash of blue light a gada bird appears in Emperor form, flying out from it. As it does so the torches on the walls flicker now with a rainbow of coloured fire.<br /> <br /> The Garda bird has been trapped here for centuries, perhaps millennia. In that time it has read every volume and scroll from the great library. It wishes now to be free from this prison. Unable to kill itself for being reborn, and unable to break free from the stone walls.<br /> <br /> Even still, it is proud, and will do its best to give up as little as possible, not even revealing its desire to be free to the players, and hoping to get them to promise it more than just freedom. It also hates the exalted for constructing this prison, even if it's its own fault for ending up here. Intimacies are<br /> <br /> Defining I Will be Free<br /> Major The Knowledge I've Gained Sets Me Apart<br /> Minor Exalted (hatred)<br /> Minor Anyone can Fall in a Trap<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 7 Escape</b><br /> <br /> If the players agree to help free it then it will lead them through the archway and to the library within. At the back, through many great stone shelves filled with ancient manuscripts there is one puzzle that the Garda bird could never figure out. A set of stone levers that supposedly when put in the right configuration will somehow raise the whole temple up a hundred feet. Either with some religious purpose or simply a show of skill by the creators.<br /> <br /> In actuality the GaDa Bird has long since figured out what position to put the levers in, a difficulty 3 Int + Lore roll determines that, but the mechanism is broken. Repairing it is a Superior repair project that requires at least a day to complete. Upon completion the temple raises up out of the deep pool that it had been sitting in and the players may leave with their prize.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I hope to do at least a couple more of these. I can make them a bit more complicated, but unfortunately I don't think I can make them much more simple. If they're going to be remotely challenging they need to be about this complex and involved, but if you're doing 3-5 hour sessions you should be able to get through one of these, or at least almost get through one. I also don't think every session needs to be like this, you can have some with more downtime obviously, and less structured, but having at least a couple like this will make your players feel justified in the charms they took not being complete overkill. Hopefully.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T20:19:06-07:00","id":1395214,"title":"","content":"Oh, nice! You mind if the rest of us also contribute stuff to the thread?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8959-prototype00","name":"prototype00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8959_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"964"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-08T20:22:23-07:00","id":1395217,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>prototype00</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1395197-adventure-ho?p=1395214#post1395214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, nice! You mind if the rest of us also contribute stuff to the thread?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah sure go ahead!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-09T09:32:02-07:00","id":1395374,"title":"","content":"These are great \u2013 thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24647-moss-reynholm","name":"Moss Reynholm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24647_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-10T18:04:11-07:00","id":1395710,"title":"","content":"Here's another one for more advanced groups that have a bit more of a combat bent to them. The premise is that Octavian is having a new fortress palace constructed, almost completed, all the fortifications are finished he's just having the finishing touches added to some parts. He also has done something, or has something the PCs need, and they're coming to take it from him by brute force. Also, this new fortress is carved into an active volcano on the surface of hell.<br /> <br /> This is an idea of what an adventure segment might look like for a party where the characters are a bit more combat oriented, but still want a challenge to their skills in combat. If combats take anywhere over an hour for your group, this may not be appropriate. Most of the combats appear daunting, but very powerful Solars should end them in 1-2 rounds. To encourage that kind of aggressive play, I suggest being very aggressive with the NPCs as well, particularly from a charm/willpower standpoint.<br /> <br /> <br /> It also has some custom creatures<br /> <br /> <br /> Smiling Peltasts are first circle demons of Octavian, but were not always named so. Long ago they were expressive demons, named Jovyn the Musical Players. They favoured putting on great shows and works of artistry. Above all though, they are unfailingly loyal to Octavian. For as long as they've been in Hell however, Octavian has demanded more of them. He has made deals with other demons of the realm to exchange the powerful expressions of the Players in exchange for twisting them into a fighting force, until now only their manic smiles remain affixed to their faces. They have also twisted their love of beautiful colours into a love for beautiful poisonous creatures and plants like arrow frogs, who then adorn their javelins.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Essence 2<br /> Willpower: 5<br /> Join Battle: 8 dice<br /> Health Levels: -0/-1x2/-2x2/-4/I<br /> <br /> Actions: Senses 8 dice, Stealth 7 dice, Resist Poison 6 dice, threaten 7 dice<br /> <br /> <b>Combat</b><br /> Javalin: 11 dice at Close range (Damage 13, minimum 2)<br /> Grapple: 7 dice (7 to control)<br /> Combat Movement 8 dice<br /> Evasion 5, parry 3<br /> Soak/Hardness 7/0<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><b>Offensive Charms</b></i><br /> <br /> Principle of Motion (10m 1wp) reflexive<br /> The Peltast can flurry two actions without penalty-it may even flurry two of the same action.<br /> <br /> Satchel of Poison(8m) Reflexive<br /> The Peltast may reflexively poison one of the javelins it has in its hands. Typically they have Arrow Frog venom, but on their travels many can pick up stranger poisons, like Anhule poison or even Yozi Venom in extreme cases. Peltast battlegroups are assumed to have poison on their attacks against crashed targets.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Commanders</b><br /> <br /> Most units of Peltasts have a commander, who is differs by having one extra attack and damage die, and a command pool of 9 dice. They also have the following charm<br /> <br /> Gift of Command (15m 1wp) supplemental<br /> <br /> Once per scene a Smiling Peltast commander can supplement an order with this charm, for one brief moment remembering the glorious expression and perfection of movement from their history. Any battlegroup rolling to execute that order may roll twice and take the higher result.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Grandmother Oghooda is a massive anhule, made such by excessive feasting on other demons, including her kin.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Her stats are similar to other Anhule except:<ul><li>she adds 2 dice to all her attack and damage rolls, as well as her grapple control roll, which she rolls unopposed against creatures smaller than her who don't have magic to grapple larger creatures.</li> <li>She has the Legendary Size merit, which also grants her an extra two -0, -1, and -2 health levels</li> <li>She has 12 soak</li> <li>She is Essence 4</li> </ul><br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> The actual adventure, starting just outside the fortress. At this point the players may still be a bit tired from their trek through hell, but I'm assuming they're fresh.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1-1 On Ground</b><br /> <br /> The approach to Octavian's palace is a daunting one. Surrounding the outside is a wall, 100 feet high, and set into that wall, a fortress gate requiring a Strength 10, difficulty 20 Feat of Strength roll to force open. It can also be cleared with charms like Feather Foot Style or Incense Smoke Ladder, but doing so requires two turns of movement, such is its height. The lock mechanism can also be opened from the inside. If a player can get to the other side of the gate, there is a gatehouse with a difficulty 3 locked door, inside that is a lever which can open the gate.<br /> <br /> <br /> Either way, behind the gate lies a network of entrenched defenders. The front line is within Short range of the other side of the gate, and consists of a size 3 battlegroup of bloodapes that fill the street, behind them and raised up on a fortified balcony are two Smiling Peltasts and a commander who oversee the battlefield. They enjoy the benefits of heavy cover from their position.<br /> <br /> After the forces are defeated reinforcements will be there shortly, so the characters must move quickly into the heart of the fortress.<br /> <br /> If the players elect to dash across the rooftops, instead of through the street, then adjust scene 1-2 to have the Peltasts swarm them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 1-2 Over Ground</b><br /> <br /> The players may wish to get past the whole outer defenses by taking to the air. Wide open in the sky Octavian's agata mounted forces all over the city can take flight and swarm the characters. A size 5 battlegroup of agata mounted peltasts and a pair of commanders rise up to darken the skies and meet the players. They focus on disabling the players' method of flight, unhorsing them from their mounts or trying to disable their vehicle.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 1-3 Underground</b><br /> <br /> A survival + perception of difficulty 5 reveals a flaw in Octavian's new fortress. There is a small cleft in the mountain which shows the barest hint of a tunnel network that leads deeper into the mountain. If the players make it there without being seen they can start to navigate to Octavian's throneroom.<br /> <br /> The tunnels are sweltering however, and endlessly labyrinthine. Navigating it requires an extended Survival + Perception roll of target number 20, and difficulty 3, the interval is one hour, and with a terminus of 4. At each interval test for environmental effects against the oppressive heat and brimstone, difficulty 3 with 3L damage.<br /> <br /> If the roll isn't completed by the terminus a minor eruption engulfs the tunnels in magma, test again against Lava and the characters are ejected to near their starting point. If the players can somehow anchor themselves and ride out the magma they can continue, keeping their successes and resetting the terminus.<br /> <br /> Proceed to scene 3 at the end of this scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Smashing Through</b><br /> <br /> The doors to Octavian's inner tower-keep are sealed down. Breaking through them is a strength 7 feat with difficulty 14. Alternatively if the players can reach one of the high windows, it can be pulled open by a Strength 5, difficulty 9 feat, or a difficulty 3 lockpicking check.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Empire's Shadows</b><br /> <br /> As the players dash into the fortress keep they run into a small group of Smiling Peltasts, each character rolls Dexterity + Combat Ability against difficulty 2 or takes a die of lethal damage. One person among that group though is not a Peltast, but a human in rope handcuffs. He has the stats of an Assassin/Spymaster, but with an extra die to social influence. He tells the players he was captured by the demons after making a bad deal with one that an associate if his summoned, and that he knows a shortcut to Octavian's throneroom.<br /> <br /> This is only half true, he was captured, but that was a decade ago. He has since fallen in love with Octavian and his empire, and is willing to give his life for it. This 'shortcut' he speaks of is actually a path into the magma tubes beneath the keep, where he plans to lead the players. If successful in persuading them, he leads them deep down into a magma tunnel, one which dead-ends. At the last moment a successful Survival + Intelligence roll of difficulty 3 reveals the death trap and lets the players flee if they choose. Otherwise when they reach the bottom the spymaster kneels down and accepts his fate as all characters are subjected to Uncountable Damage from thousands of tons of molten rock.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 Workplace Hazards</b><br /> <br /> Outside of Octavian's throneroom is a large amount of construction equipment, treadmill operated cranes with golems for power, ladders, and cut stones littered all over. The work crews flee as soon as the players arrive, but hidden in the unfinished ceiling is Oghooda and four of her smaller Anhule kin waiting to ambush the characters.<br /> <br /> The fleeing work crews though did not leave their workplace in good shape. Golems continue to wind the cables, and unsupervised stone blocks now teeter precariously on their scaffolding. At the end of each round, after everyone regenerates motes, make a difficulty 3 environmental hazard roll with Dexterity + Dodge and damage 2B from snapping cables or falling brickwork.<br /> <br /> After Oghooda falls, the shockwaves of that impact harmlessly snap and wiggle loose any remaining construction equipment, ending the effect.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Parlay</b><br /> <br /> At that though, the doors to the Throne of Octavian are all that remain. The players find them unlocked, and when they push it open it reveals a massive room, filled with the snarling and grunting of over a hundred bloodapes. At the far side sits Octavian, astride his Agata, and beside him stands a figure in tarnished Orichalcum fullplate. This figure is Arynae, a Fiend caste Infernal exalt, use the stats of the Wandering Deathknight but replace their charms with White Reaper Style, including Mastery effects. She is also Essence 3. Arynaae was visiting Octavian to ask a favour. When the attack started Octavian asked her for her support here as payment.<br /> <br /> He will try to bargain with the players for what they want, whatever it is here that might be. If no acceptable deal can be made, combat is joined<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Death of an Emperor</b><br /> <br /> In combat Octavian charges into battle, flurrying attacks using Principle of Motion, either with more attacks or with command rolls to his bloodapes. He builds up initiative with witering attacks, and counters enemies with Superior Strength Reversal. While his Agata is on the ground he gains the benefits of being a mounted combatant, but these are lost if he takes to the air.<br /> <br /> For added challenge rather than rolling in Octavian's Agata, add a Peltast commander to oversee the bloodapes.<br /> <br /> After Octavian's death the remaining forces surrender to the players.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-11T13:54:51-07:00","id":1395906,"title":"","content":"This one is a bit of a twist on a fairy snow queen and stolen children. I would be surprised if a group managed to get through all of this in one session, but I think it would still be worth doing even stretched over two sessions.<br /> <br /> <br /> The premise of this is that a town in the north has experienced a problem. A snowstorm has rolled in and sat over the town for two days, and during this time one of the town's young children has gone missing. .<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Investigation</b><br /> <br /> At the scene of the kidnapping, the child's bedroom, the players can make a Case Scene roll, difficulty 4 to uncover evidence. That roll reveals a shred of cloth and a footprint that indicates the child himself walked up to the window and unlatched it, and then climbed out.<br /> <br /> In addition to this players may want to Read Intentions some of the towns people. Doing so reveals that they are hiding something. Leveraging at least a Minor intimacy will get them to reveal that they they have a legend of an cold fey queen who lives in a palace of ice on top of a mountain to the north, and that in times past she has come down from there to steal children away.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Snowstorm</b><br /> <br /> To get through the storm to the supposed ice palace make an extended Perception + Survival roll of difficulty 3 and goal number of 15. No terminus but the interval is one hour, and every time all characters must test against extreme cold.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Whiteout</b><br /> <br /> As the players reach the top of the mountain they find the storm progressing quickly to a complete whiteout. More than that, the wind is picking up and flinging razor sharp shards of ice from the ground. Immidiately test against Supernatural Icestorm. There is a glimmer of hope to make it through though, three white horses appear out of the storm, standing not in the snow but atop it.<br /> <br /> These are Aughdeighe, and will attempt to coax the characters into touching them, and then use Hold Fast to trap them and flee into the storm. The terrain here is difficult due to the incredibly thick snow, but the Aughdeighe do not suffer from it.<br /> <br /> If an Aughdeighe does successfully withdraw with a character they are not killed, but instead captured by the palace.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 Palace entrance</b><br /> <br /> Finding a way through the supernatural ice storm to the shelter of the palace calls for a Difficulty 4 Perception + Survival roll. Each failure prompts another test against the ice storm's environmental damage.<br /> <br /> On a success the characters reach the main door of the palace, a towering structure of stone and ice which rises up into the whiteout storm. The door is sorcerously locked with a difficulty 5 lock. It can also be smashed through with a strength 7+ difficulty 12 Feat of Demolition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Main Hall</b><br /> <br /> The main hall is a beautiful structure of glass or ice buttresses, statues and tapestries depicting epic adventures of northern people and monsters. At the rear is a massive spiral staircase that goes both up and down. Intruders into the palace are met by Ingolin, the Cataphract head of the palace guard, a size 2 battlegroup of hobgoblins, and a Buck-Ogre enforcer. All die normally except for Ingolin, who when slain seems to melt into a puddle of water.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Aside Dungeons</b><br /> <br /> Down the staircase is the palace dungeons, a prison for those captured by the aughdeighe or the fey creatures of the palace. The warden is a particularly intelligent, if lazy and cowardly, hobgoblin who feeds off the fear of his prisoners. If confronted he attempts to barter for his life by opening the cells for the players. Although the child they seek is not in them, anyone carried off in scene 3 will be.<br /> <br /> A side passage to this area also houses the quarters and living space for the palace guards.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 The Playroom</b><br /> <br /> At the top of the spiral staircase is a floor of the palace dedicated to what appears to be a children's play area. It is filled with toys, small gymnastic structures, stuffed animals, life-size toy soldiers, and enormous fur bags filled with seeds or dry beans. Casing the scene at a difficulty of 2 reveals that rather than just haphazardly discarded some of the toys look deliberately hurled or broken, as through in a fit of rage.<br /> <br /> If anyone crosses more than halfway through the room, the toy soldiers come to life and assault the players. Treat them as five walking statues, but their wind up springs only have so much strength left in them. At the end of turn 3, before the turn mote regeneration, they all slow to a halt.<br /> <br /> At the far side lies another staircase upwards.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 7 The Wardrobe</b><br /> <br /> The next floor up greets the players with a wall of outfits, dresses, suits, shoes, hats, and all manner of garments. They sit on shelves, racks, in rotating closets and in some cases are piled up just on the floor. The whole thing is a maze, and one that appears to be too big to fit even in this massive structure.<br /> <br /> Moving through this maze is fairly simple, but there are spirits nearby, if the characters have the charms to detect them. Seeing the spirits reveals them to be a pair of children, ghosts of previous victims of the palace. Use the stats of an honoured ancestor.<br /> <br /> Intimacies:<br /> <br /> Defining Princess of the Palace (Fear)<br /> Major Eachother (friendship)<br /> Major Nobody should be Alone<br /> Minor I still miss the games we played<br /> <br /> <br /> They can be convinced to appear and tell the characters basically what is going on. The princess of the castle is either a fey creature, or a mortal child tainted by fey energy. She lives here with her small group of palace attendants in relative luxury, but none of them really make her happy. The goblins are ugly and brutish, Ingolin is distant and gruff, and her Lorelei, Feria, is motherly but not fun. When she can't stand the loneliness or boredom anymore she convinces Feria to go out into the world and bring her a child to play with.<br /> <br /> She doesn't know how to properly care for mortal children though, and they inevitably end up wasting away through lack of food, proper shelter, or die in desperate attempts to escape.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 8 The Bedroom</b><br /> <br /> Guided by the ghosts, or just through perseverance, the characters make it to the last staircase leading to the bedroom. It's massive, again taking up most of the floor, and includes a large bed, a puppet theater, and several long tables. Sat on the bed are Feria, stroking the hair of a small girl, and beside them a small boy who looks scared. At the table sits Ingolin, although wounded he somehow survived his death.<br /> <br /> Feria bids the characters not to be too loud, or they'll wake up her princess. She then tries to seduce the characters, or convince them that what she's doing is right, and that she knows what she did wrong, and that her new kidnapped child will have a full life of joy and endless safe adventure. This is a lie, Feria has always known what mortal children need to live, but she always grows jealous of her princess when she seems to take more of a liking to her new friend than to Faria. She then engineers for that problem to be solved one way or another.<br /> <br /> Her princess is actually a human child, the first one she ever stole, and through the use of Wyld magic she keeps her eternally young.<br /> <br /> Intimacies are<br /> <br /> Defining Princess (Love)<br /> Major Princess's friends (jealousy)<br /> Major Ingonlin (respect)<br /> Minor I know what's best for everyone I meet<br /> <br /> <br /> Ingolin's intimacies are<br /> Defining Princess (Loyalty)<br /> Major None will breach this palace while I live<br /> Minor Feria (suspicion)<br /> <br /> <br /> Feria is not much of a fighter, if the players decide just to slay her there's no need to run the combat, she will stay to defend her princess until the end. Unless Ingolin can be convinced otherwise, he will join her side, but he has all of his -1 health levels still filled with damage, so is not much of a threat. </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-12T13:55:57-07:00","id":1396068,"title":"","content":"I TOTALLY thought this thread was about something else.<br /> <br /> Still... I have a couple of ideas about scenes with intrepid nemoah that might still be appropriate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-06-15T06:59:21-07:00","id":1396671,"title":"","content":"Also a tip on running NPC Essence users. In most cases you don\u2019t need to track their motes at all. A Dog of the Unbroken Earth can literally never run out of motes in combat no matter how hard it tries. A bloodape also basically can\u2019t, Anhule can technically but they have to constantly be using their mobility charms combined with stuff like principle of motion and it still takes like 5 rounds if you\u2019re trying hard to burn motes as hard as possible.<br /> <br /> The only people who can reliably burn motes fast enough to matter are people with excellency like charms, such as Exalted or Octavian or the like.<br /> <br /> Even willpower doesn\u2019t need to be tracked unless you\u2019re being pretty liberal with it. If you\u2019re fielding a buck-ogre and having it spend a willpower to flurry two attacks, and spend a willpower on each of their attacks, and spend a willpower on his own defence when he gets attacked, that\u2019s when you\u2019re going to want to keep track of it. If you\u2019re just using a Principle of Motion here, or a willpower on an attack rom there, and they\u2019re nameless QC-NPCs who will die by the end of the scene or will never be seen again, you can be almost certain you won\u2019t be overspending on willpower. Also if occasionally you overspend on willpower by one doing this it\u2019s not the end of the world, that particular Disease Spirit just has 6 willpower instead of 5, it happens. <br /> <br /> <br /> The only thing you really need to keep track of is health levels, and you can do that just like you would in DnD. The only thing you might want is to have their wound penalty breakpoints somewhere on your paper. For example a dog of the unbroken earth has them at -1 at 2+, -2 at 8+, and -4 at 14+. Which looks hard to read in text but you can make yourself a really simple little chart on actual paper that looks way better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T15:49:27-07:00","id":1406519,"title":"","content":"Here I have another adventure taking place around a massive ebony pyramid in the south. It has in it an ancient undead pharaoh who plans after all these millenia to use the power of the pyramid to poison the dragon lines and unleash a plague of undead monsters on his former kingdom, retaking it. The players will have to contend with the defenses of the pyramid, the forces of the old pharaoh, and a disease that turns your blood to sand.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dune Rot<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> A disease that desiccates the body and eventually turns the blood to sand. Virulence 4, Morbidity 5, vector airborne.<br /> <br /> Minor Symptom: The victim has a great thirst and skin, especially around the lips, eyes, and nose, begin to dry out. Subtract 2 from the number of days the character can survive without water, minimum 1.<br /> <br /> Major Symptom: The dryness increases, now spreading to the skin of the palms, and torso, becoming white and cracked. When a player suffers a botch from this disease, they also suffer from a -2 wound penalty for the remainder of the scene, or the next scene if it occurs right near the end.<br /> <br /> Defining Symptom: The character is now dry and cracked all over, any lethal wounds they suffer bleed out bloody sand instead of fluid. The wound penalty is now permanent, and when they suffer a botch it doubles all of their wound penalties for the scene.<br /> <br /> Death: Gasping out their last puffs of sandy air, the victim's body contorts into a corpse<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> High Priests<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> The undead priests of the old kingdom were legendary in their rhetoric and philosophy when they were alive, and have only grown more so in their death. Their social stats are as follows.<br /> <br /> Personal Motes: 20<br /> Willpower: 7<br /> Read Intentions 12 dice; Social Influence 14 dice; Senses 8 dice<br /> Resolve 6, Guile 4<br /> <br /> <br /> Merits/charms<br /> <br /> Dead Philosophers: The High Priests are not affected in either direction by various appearance scores.<br /> <br /> Dreams of Nostalgia: 5m 1wp, Supplemental. When a priest rolls to inspire the emotion of Nostalgia they conjure a ghostly image on the table that infiltrates the mind of any who see it. The effect is that they feel nostalgic for this memory of the priest, even if they themselves have never seen it. Remembering their glory days as if they were their own<br /> <br /> Preaching Divinity: 10m 1wp, Supplemental. When a priest raises their voice and preaches as though to ten thousand it is nearly impossible to resist. A social influence attempt enhanced by this charm ignores the penalty for targeting multiple people, as well as granting the priest double 9s on the roll.<br /> <br /> Rhetoric of Ages: 5m, Reflexive. When applying resolve against Instill actions the high priest may reflexively use this charm to raise their Resolve by 2.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> Iscarus Champions<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> A small but elite family of beetle-folk who served the pharaoh as his most skilled and powerful champions. Combat stats as follows:<br /> <br /> Willpower: 5<br /> Join battle: 10 dice<br /> Health levels: -0/-1x4/-2x4/-4/I<br /> Command: 10 dice<br /> Long Axe: 11 dice (Damage 16L)<br /> Evasion 2 Parry 4<br /> Soak 12 (Hardened shell)<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> Scenes in this adventure<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Sandtrap</b><br /> <br /> A local nomad sits atop a camel at the peak of a far dune. He's made a deal with a massive Steel Eater. He sits nearby and acts as bait, either for travelers wanting to give/receive help or bandits looking for an easy mark. When they cross the foot of the dune they fall into a sand trap and have their metal items devoured by the steel eater, any valuable non-metals go to the nomad.<br /> <br /> This Steel Eater is larger than normal with an extra health level of each grade, and with four extra damage on its bite. It also has up to four normal sized steel eaters. It sometimes sends them off to collect metal from distant places, but can have all four in its pit with it.<br /> <br /> It has a major intimacy of being possessive of its small Steel Eater group, and a minor one of fondness for the nomad it works with.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Colourful Tents</b><br /> <br /> The nomad, either impressed by the player's strength in fighting off the Steel Eater, cunning in convincing it to let them go, or wisdom in seeing the trap and avoid it, asks them to join him and come to meet the head of his tribe.<br /> <br /> About an hour's travel through the desert away is a large group of colourful tents, most small, but a few are very large and extravagant. Looming on the horizon far past the tents is a massive black stone pyramid. Being taken to the most impressive tent the characters meet the tribe's leader, with the stats of a Snake Folk Merchant, with the following intimacies<br /> <br /> Defining &quot;My people have never had a better leader than I&quot;<br /> Major &quot;I will live up to my fore-bearer's legacy&quot;<br /> Major The Ebony Pyramid (Fear)<br /> Minor Alcohol (addiction)<br /> <br /> <br /> He begs the players to investigate the Ebony Pyramid, a great structure in the area which is rumored to bring about the end of the world at the return of Garin's Comet, which has again appeared in the sky. He can be convinced to give the players a guide , but doing so is a serious task or with an appropriate bargain, as he is loathe to send any of his people to what he believes is a deadly place.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 Crossing the Desert</b><br /> <br /> Crossing the desert during the day is an extended Survival + Wits roll, difficulty 0, goal number 10, with an interval of one hour. At the end of each interval the players suffer environmental damage from extreme heat. This can be stopped by waiting for 8pm, but this increases the difficulty of the extended roll to 2 as it is new moon, and according to their stories at Midnight the doom will arrive.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 Getting Into the Pyramid</b><br /> <br /> The players eventually arrive at their destination they find that the pyramid has only one entrance. Above it is a message written in some ancient dialect of Firetongue, deciphering it is an Intelligence + Linguistics roll of difficulty 3. Success reveals that this pyramid represents the last bastion of power of an ancient southern dynasty, and it has been placed under a curse. All who breach it will suffer a terrible wasting curse.<br /> <br /> An Intelligence + Medicine roll of difficulty 4 reveals through the flowery ancient language that this is actually a magical disease known as Dune Rot. One that causes your skin to dry into scaly, flaky husk, and their blood to sand. From now on each scene where the players open a new area unless otherwise noted, they test against Dune Rot, however lower the Virulence to 1 if they have most of their bodies, including face, wrapped in cloth.<br /> <br /> Breaking through the door requires either a Feat of Demolition of Strength 10, difficulty 15, a Dex + Larceny test at difficulty 4, or through proper use of the key. Failure to lockpick it or success at breaking through it sends an alert to the nephwrack-pharoh deep in the tomb.<br /> <br /> The key may be connected to another story, or perhaps in the possession of the Steel Eater from earlier.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Farmer's Hall</b><br /> <br /> This room does not contain Dune Rot<br /> <br /> Upon opening the tomb the players come upon a hall dedicated to honouring the spirits of the farmers who provided for the laborers of the pyramid. A mural of the farmers tending the irrigated fields covers the back wall. There is no apparent exit, however a difficulty 3 Perception + Awareness roll reveals that the floor and walls have been swept or washed away from the mural towards the door, flowing past the pillars.<br /> <br /> At that moment the stone mural splits along the seam of the large river and from it pours a torrent of water. Anyone in the room must succeed at a difficulty 4 Strength + Athletics roll or be thrown a range band back. Lower that to difficulty 2 if the character had warning and is using the pillars to hide behind the flow. A botch on this roll represents being pulled into the undertow, after which the character begins to drown. A character can hold another in place, although this itself requires a feat of strength of difficulty 3.<br /> <br /> To advance up the room requires a difficulty 6 feat of strength, or some other inventive solution to the problem. After passing through the doorway there is a lever to stop the torrent. This also closes and re-locks the exterior door, and anyone currently outside it is trapped there by it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Soldier's Hall</b><br /> <br /> After a short ramp upward the players move deeper into the pyramid. The archway at the end of the ramp details this as the floor honouring the soldiers of the kingdom. Pushing open the unlocked stone doors reveals a hallway with large statues of beetle-men. These depict the pharoh's iscarus beetle warriors, fabled beastmen champions of the kingdom. The door on the far side is barricaded from opening by two such statues.<br /> <br /> As the players enter the room they hear a chittering sound and six of the statues's mouths split open, pouring endless flesh-eating beetles out onto the floor. They have the stats of golems. Four of them are within Close range of the entrance, and two at Short range, at the far end of the hall. The ones at the far end use greatbows, accuracy 10, 15 damage, minimum 2.<br /> <br /> The beetles flooding the room cause the ground to count as difficult terrain. Also at the end of each round the players must roll a difficulty 3 environmental hazard with 1L damage, as the insects swarm, and fly onto exposed skin, or burrow between armored plates and thick clothing to get to flesh.<br /> <br /> <br /> The two statues blockading the door are animated, but unresponsive to combat. Anyone who approaches prompts them to lean forward and inspect the players, saying after that only nobility may pass. They can be destroyed by a Strength 7, difficulty 10 feat of strength, as they do not resist.<br /> <br /> They may also be convinced that the players are, indeed, nobility, by overcoming their perception + investigation pool, 12 dice, using a disguise. They may also be convinced through social pressure. They have a resolve of 6, and the following intimacies<br /> <br /> Defining Pharaoh (Loyalty)<br /> Major intimacy of pride in the Iscarus Beetle warriors<br /> Major intimacy that &quot;The priests of this land are wise beond all.&quot;<br /> <br /> Convincing the statues that the players are nobility without any kind of disguise or evidence is taken at a -5 penalty, and with some evidence at -3.<br /> <br /> <b>Scene 7 Priest's Chamber</b><br /> <br /> Past the door and down another winding series of hallways the players hear voices coming from the next room. The door here is open, and behind it lies a great stone table, with two figures sitting at each end. Their bodies resemble dessicated corpses, chained to their stone chairs, although the chains rattle and shake as they gesticulate along with their philisophical debate. Surrounding them in the dark corners of the room are a crowd of lesser undead philosopher-priests, listening to them.<br /> <br /> They are the Upper and Lower priests of the old kingdom. The Upper priest is arguing that after the pyramid is opened and the dragon lines have been used to purge the land, that it should be the undead subjects who populate this new empire. The Lower priest rejects this notion, that of course the old undead lineage must rule, but it must rule over the living, and indeed if the bloodline can again be found perhaps a new living pharoh can take their place.<br /> <br /> If the players do not interrupt them, they pay them no mind and can simply bypass this room. If they choose to engage in debate, however, the priests welcome them to the table. They have the following intimacies:<br /> <br /> <br /> Upper Priest<br /> <br /> Defining &quot;Knowledge is an end all to itself&quot;<br /> Major Pharaoh (Loyalty)<br /> Major &quot;The old Kingdom was the height of civilization&quot;<br /> Major &quot;In rulership blood is not as important as skill&quot;<br /> Major Lower Priest (Deep Respect)<br /> Minor Lesser Priests (Fatherly Love)<br /> <br /> <br /> Lower Priest<br /> <br /> Defining &quot;Knowledge is an end all to itself&quot;<br /> Major Pharaoh (Loyalty)<br /> Major &quot;The old Kingdom was the height of civilization&quot;<br /> Major &quot;Blood Lineage provides necessary stability to the kingdom&quot;<br /> Major Upper Priest (Deep Respect)<br /> Minor Lesser Priests (Motherly Love)<br /> <br /> <br /> If the players can convince the priests that the pyramid should not be opened, then they will provide the players with a secret back entrance to the Army of the Gods. One which drops them right before the pharaoh. Otherwise the priests attempt to convince the players that the world would be better off, ultimately, if the temple was opened.<br /> <br /> If the players attack the priests or otherwise really make them angry the pharaoh is warned of their intrusion.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 8 Pharaoh's Treasury</b><br /> <br /> This room does not contain Dune Rot<br /> <br /> Past this the hallway there are two archways, one leading to the army of the gods, and the other to the pharaoh's treasury. The treasury door is down a short passage, and is itself a giant glittering golden edifice. It is locked by a difficulty 3 lock. However upon opening this lock the thin golden door splits and slides apart, revealing another locked door just beyond, and some kind of mechanical ticking sound. Each player now has time to take two actions, if the second door is unlocked, difficulty 5 this time, a third door opens and the ticking speeds up. This door is difficulty 6 and only allows one action. When opened the ticking stops to reveal the treasure room.<br /> <br /> If at any point a door is not opened the entire floor drops away, dropping the players three range bands into a pool of acid. See the optional scene.<br /> <br /> The treasure room not only contains enormous wealth, but also priceless cultural treasures from ages ago, and various powerful items at the storyteller's discretion.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 9 Army of the Gods</b><br /> <br /> At last the players reach the center of the pyramid, an opening of a grand spiral staircase wending its way downward. As they move through it they come to a spot where the wall has a window. Peering through it reveals a breathtaking scene. A massive underground cavern, supported by hundreds of massive columns, and filled with tens of thousands of undead soldiers, horses, chariots, monstrosities and animated constructs. At the center of it all, and beneath them, is a massive raised platform. On it stand 1,000 of the most heavily armed and ornamented soldiers, as well as standing on a raised dais in the middle is the figure of the Pharaoh, flanked by two undead Iscarus champions.<br /> <br /> If the players convinced the priests, the staircase they come down comes down right onto the dais, otherwise they come down at the very edge of the raised platform, at Medium range from the dais. Falling from the raised platform counts as a Short range fall.<br /> <br /> The Pharaoh has the stats of a Nephwrack, the Iscarus champions are detailed above, and the elite firstborn guard have the stats of elite soldiers, with elite drill and perfect morale. If the Pharaoh has been warned of this intrusion he activates Dawn of Dead Stars and Clinging Shadow Miasma as the players enter the staircase.<br /> <br /> On the dais the Pharaoh is attempting to complete the ritual to open the pyramid and poison the dragon lines. If he is not slain or otherwise incapacitated here, he will do so at midnight. He knows the spells Death of Obsidian Butterflies, Mists of Eventide, Virtuous Guardian of Flame, and Impervious Sphere of Water<br /> <br /> If the Pharaoh is slain his undead army continues to fight on, although none but his champions and his elite force are intelligent enough to take initiative doing so. After midnight the magic in the temple gutters out, and the forces down here return to eternal rest.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Optional Scene Acid Pit</b><br /> <br /> At the failure of the treasure door, or any other time during the exploration where the storyteller has made their own room which may warrant it, the players drop into a pit of acid. The pool has the traits of an acid bath, and fills the whole room. A door can be found on on one side, breaching it is a difficulty 4 Dex + Larceny check, or a strength 7+ Difficulty 10 feat of demolition. On the other side is a lever which, when pulled, causes the acid to drain and a set of stone stairs to extend from the wall, allowing ascent back to where the players came from.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> It has been pointed out to me that in scene 1 the players may want to slay the nomad who was acting as bait. My thought was that Steel Eaters are not particularly dangerous, so most parties may see the trap as merely an inconvenience. If they want to kill him the nomad begs for his life and says his people is in need of great women and men just now. A Read Intentions roll of difficulty 2 reveals that his intention here is indeed to return to his home with help.<br /> <br /> If after that the players decide to slay him and not seek his people, then at midnight the baleful energies of the underworld are injected into the dragon lines, causing the elements in the area to writhe and die, and unbeknownst to the players the great ebony pyramid's sides crack open and vomit forth ancient legions of the dead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","user":"DrLoveMonkey","date":". 08-06-2020, 01:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T21:29:07-07:00","id":1406898,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve been trying to complete the Ebony Pyramid for a while now but haven\u2019t kicked myself into action until recently, so I\u2019m just making this post to make sure it doesn\u2019t take me another three weeks to finish Ghosts of the Imperial Treasury.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-27T20:58:02-07:00","id":1415779,"title":"","content":"Ghosts of the imperial treasury is about a long dead rivalry and history repeating itself. Long ago in the treasury a woman worked hard at finding embezzled money and opening vaults who's owners had died or refused to allow open. Unfortunately a rival of hers who was jealous of this success arranged for her death, and was subsequently killed by her family in revenge. Now in a case of history repeating itself a young dynastic functionary is having her career ruined by a rival. Midnight tonight marks her last day on the job, and ruination for her household.<br /> <br /> She's also been plagued by ghostly apparitions for months and has asked that the players help her investigate them, as it may be her last chance to know. If all goes well she will be credited for opening one of the oldest sealed vaults, the door of 109 eyes. A massive brass door with inset 109 mechanical eyes that creepily watch anyone who stands before them. Inside is enough recovered treasure to save her career. <br /> <br /> The basic premise is that this game starts at 2pm and goes the 10 hours until midnight. No need to worry about the treasury closing, it works through the night, albeit at a slower pace. The main places that the characters can lose time are at 3 starts of scenes, where 1 hour is marked off. They can also lose an hour to bureaucratic processes when they request information, and in two extended rolls with a duration of 1 hour. The first being to modernize an old address, and the second to find a journal in a pile of paper.<br /> <br /> If the players don't get the job done by midnight the Treasury will thank them for their discovery, possibly still give them the finders fee, but their friend will still be sacked. Rules are rules after all.<br /> <br /> The Keeper<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Essence 3<br /> Aspect: Earth<br /> <br /> 10 dice to social influence +4 dice for 5m<br /> 5 Resolve +2 for 4m<br /> 2 Appearance<br /> 4 Intelligence<br /> <br /> Social charms<br /> Frozen Heart Prana<br /> Heart-Hardening Meditation<br /> <br /> Eternally Argumentative Flame<br /> Grinding Millstone Argument<br /> <br /> <br /> Intimacies<br /> Defining Treasury (Loyalty)<br /> Major &quot;Gatekeeping is a noble profession, no matter what the others say&quot;<br /> Major &quot;Solitude is a gift I treasure&quot;<br /> Minor &quot;Sometimes I wonder if my colleagues forget I'm down here...&quot;<br /> Minor Things Within the Overflow Floor (Fear)<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Scene 1 Secret Closet</b><br /> <br /> In the functionary's office she says she's seen the ghost lingering near her closet, a Perception + Investigation roll of difficulty 2 reveals at the back of the closet is a secret, hidden door. If difficulty 5 is also met it reveals a key melded into the wainscoting.<br /> <br /> Lockpicking the door without the key is difficulty 3, forcing it open with a feat of demolition is a Strength 5+ difficulty 6 feat. This room is filled with boxes and shelves of paperwork. Conspicuously, on the table, there is a hastily scribbled note that only reads &quot;238JD1-A1 IMPORTANT&quot;. An Intelligence + Bureaucracy roll of difficulty 3 reveals this to be an old, and outdated, filing system location.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 2 Request for Information - Mark off 1 hour</b><br /> <br /> Translating the old style address into a usable modern one is an extended Intelligence + Bureaucracy roll with an interval of an hour, goal of 10 and difficulty 2. Once achieved they can submit a request for this file at the front desk of the archives. The person at the desk has the stats of the snakefolk merchant, with the following intimacies.<br /> <br /> Defining &quot;This place would fall apart without the work I do here&quot;<br /> Major &quot;I am well overdue for a promotion&quot;<br /> Major Husband (love)<br /> Minor Immaculate Order (Faith)<br /> Minor Alani from archive staff (professional dislike)<br /> <br /> <br /> The hero's functionary does not have authority to simply request access to this file, and submitting a request through the proper channels will take about three hours. This can be expedited with bureaucracy charms, or by convincing the clerk to allow them past, which is a Serious task as it risks her whole career. If the players engage her in social influence, she attempts to convince them that they need to just wait for the request to go through.<br /> <br /> Sneaking past is also an option, however the door to the archive is shut and guarded by a pair of Vaktri as well as the clerk. Players would have to get creative to get everyone in without anyone noticing. The archive is also unlit, if the players take light with them they risk alerting other guardian spirits, if they do not they suffer darkness penalties for the duration.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 3 The Shallow Archive-Recent Records</b><br /> <br /> After gaining access to the archive the players make their way to the location. Wending their way though wide corridors and around shelves stacked with boxes, scrolls and tablets the heroes enter a smaller room in the back where their prize supposedly rests. Opening the designated drawer they find a long and short lockbox, difficulty 3 to pick open or strength 5+ difficulty 5 feat of demolition. If the lockpicking check is failed, or if the box is smashed open, it releases a cloud of caustic acid spray into the air that affects everyone within Close range, difficulty 4 to resist and damage 3L.<br /> <br /> Inside the box is a note of garbled numbers and letters in small groups. A cypher formed by a Cryptic Essence Cypher charm, difficulty 5 Intelligence + Linguistics roll to decrypt. It says &quot;SORRY! If you find this it means you're looking for your journal scroll, but I needed to borrow it to try and unlock the golden circles door. I accidentally left it at the end storage hall in B33A, but I swear I'll get it back by next week! SORRY AGAIN!&quot;<br /> <br /> B33A is a room on one of the basement floors two flights down from where the players are now. Clearly this note has been here for a long time however.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 4 The Middle Archive-Unique Barbarian Tribute - Mark off 1 hour</b><br /> <br /> Moving through the archive and down two flights of stairs they reach the portion of the archive dedicated to recording unique forms of tribute. For example a satrapy may have been required to pay tribute in the form of 500 statues or other works, and in which case 505 would actually be shipped, and 5 would go to the archive and various other places for record keeping. Surrounding the players are suits of armour, paintings, statues, figurines, and any other odd assortment of precious things.<br /> <br /> The air also grows colder here, unnaturally so, and a Perception + Awareness roll of difficulty 2 reveals that there don't seem to be any of the elemental guardians in this section of the floor. On approaching the storage room in question a ghostly apparition of an older woman appears and throws out a hand. A perception + linguistics roll difficulty 2 tells the character she's warning of immediate danger. Either this or a Perception + Awareness roll difficulty 3 prevents the PCs from being caught by surprise.<br /> <br /> The surprise is a mortwight using Breath of the Abyss descending from the ceiling and war ghosts animating various armors around the room. All together a size 2 battlegroup of heavy armor war ghosts with a commander. The commander has similar stats but also 7 command dice. This commander is the old rival of the dead Dynast.<br /> <br /> After these are dealt with the players enter the room B33A to find it totally empty, with a note pinned to the inner door reading &quot;Area cleared for Chiaroscuro glassworks tribute RY722, all materials moved to room B69L&quot; This room is even further down, another three floors, near the bottom of the usable archive. The ghostly apparition appears again, this time sinking into the floor and beckoning.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 5 Overflow Gatekeeper</b><br /> <br /> As the players descend a few more staircases they find that at the bottom of the last flight a steel cage and gate has been erected around the exit of the stairs, with a figure sat behid a small opening beside the gate. They appear to be very old, and a profile character action of difficulty 2 reveals that they are using a jade quill and show earth aspect markings.<br /> <br /> If questioned about the gate the Keeper says that overflow security is high. Any area of the archive can experience overflow, dangerous areas, or precious areas, and thus the overflow has higher security. The Keeper is reluctant to let the players into the overflow area, both to keep what's in there safe from tampering and keep the outside safe from what's inside.<br /> <br /> The cage also has a sorcerous enchantment worked into it. Anyone attempting to attack or pass through the cage must apply Resolve against a roll of 10 dice. If the character fails then their attempt to bypass only ends up with them on the side they started from, and an attack ends with them targeting something else on their side of the cage.<br /> <br /> If the players convince the Keeper to let them through he turns a starmetal key in a lock in front of him which unlocks the door. He also notes to the players as they leave that his office is protected from the overflow archive as much as it is from the outside.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 6 Overflow Archives </b><br /> <br /> This area of the deep archive is not well organized at all. Even the architecture is haphazard, some very low wide rooms are attached by wide narrow hallways, often with short flights of steps between, or doors that must be ducked under. The contents of the rooms are likewise haphazardly placed. Piles of books, scrolls, tablets, tables, shelves, barrels, steel plates are arranged to form walkways through the larger rooms, and sometimes fill the smaller rooms to the point of having to crawl over detritus to get through them. Although labyrinthine a gentle wisp of air seems to always urge them forward.<br /> <br /> As they get through the final door they open it to reveal a massive room, piled in a slope to the ceiling with a mountain of junk, paperwork and baubles. A perception + awareness roll of difficulty 2 spots the item they're looking for. Wedged between the very top of the pile at the far back corner, and the ceiling.<br /> <br /> Reaching it over the massive pile of precariously balanced materials and slick paper-on-paper is a difficulty 5 Dexterity + Athletics roll. If failed the entire thing comes tumbling down creating an environmental hazard, difficulty 3 Dex+Dodge and 3B damage if failed. It takes one roll to go up, and another to go down.<br /> <br /> If the roll is failed going up before the journal has been grabbed then the journal is lost in the tumbling pile. Retrieving it is an extended Percep+Awareness roll with difficulty 0 and a goal number of 10. The interval is one hour. Only one player may roll but may receive an extra non-charm die for each other player searching.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 7 Not Alone Down Here - Mark off 1 hour</b><br /> <br /> As the players leave with their prize they hear loud noises, something shifting several rooms away. As they move back through the rooms they find them trashed, papers everywhere and tables overturned. Hiding in and among this are three serpentine spirits, amalgamations of ghosts who's anchors to the world have been brought here, they now possess long chains of paper and other junk and on rare occasion randomly assault people who come through here. They use the stats of the giant constrictor.<br /> <br /> The ground here is not only radically uneven, but also unstable and covered in shifting paper, so all physical actions and defenses suffer a -3 penalty. The paper serpents do not suffer from this however as they move through it easily.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Scene 8 The Door of 109 Eyes</b><br /> <br /> After dealing with the snakes and leaving the functionary looks at the journal. Finding in it something amazing, the way to open the Door of 109 eyes. A vault in the treasury that has gone unopened for ages. In the book is a very complex lullaby that if played before the door causes the eyes to fall asleep and the door to open. However if the song falters the eyes strike out with lancing beams of killing light. Sightreading the song is a difficulty 7 charisma + performance roll.<br /> <br /> If the players go and do this before midnight the door opens to reveal enormous wealth, which will save the functionary's career, and can be bargained into a 10% finders fee. Failure to play the song doesn't prevent another attempt, but does cause the eyes to ignite and lance out with deadly beams of light, a Dex+Dodge hazard of difficulty 5 and 5L to resist.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","user":"DrLoveMonkey","date":". 09-28-2020, 03:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}